Title: gaze: waive the un-reviewable breaker while still running review
Created: 2026-10-19
Author: reviewer

--- log ---
2026-10-19T21:14Z reviewer created

--- body ---
## Context
`/gaze` stops any PR of 15 or more content-bearing files before review
(`skills/gaze/SKILL.md` un-reviewable breaker). The only hatch is
`--force-approve`, which skips phase 6 and runs no reviewer battery, so an
oversized but legitimately indivisible PR (a mechanical rename and rewire, PR
1482: 24 files in one commit) must choose between an unreviewed approval and a
split that cannot clear the threshold. The breaker also fires only after the
work is done.

## Scope
1. A narrower override, `--waive-breaker <reason>`: lifts only the file-count
   stop, then runs the normal battery, simplify and verify-gate. Record the
   waived breaker, file and line counts and the reason in the PR comment and
   the verdict header, as `--force-approve` does.
2. A declaration the author can make before review (PR body line, for example
   `Review-scope: indivisible — <reason>`), honoured the same way, so the stop
   is not discovered after the work.
3. State in `skills/gaze/SKILL.md` and `rules/workflow.md` (Ticket discipline
   for multi-PR work) that the 15-file threshold is a decomposition signal
   with this hatch, not a hard wall; update the drift-pin test that restates it.
4. Make the `--force-approve` section say plainly that it runs no reviewer
   battery and no gate.

## Exit criteria
- [ ] `/gaze <pr> --waive-breaker <reason>` on an over-threshold PR runs the full battery and gate and posts the waiver
- [ ] The PR-body declaration is honoured and recorded
- [ ] workflow.md and gaze SKILL.md agree on the threshold and both hatches; the drift-pin test passes
- [ ] `--force-approve` documentation states it skips review
